<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gadaladeniya Vihara</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1344">1344</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gadaladeniya is a Buddhist temple near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kandy">Kandy</a> in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a>, built in 1344 under King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhuvanaikabahu-iv">Bhuvanaikabahu IV</a> of Gampola. Designed by a south Indian architect, its stone shrine combines Sinhalese and Dravidian styles and is one of the finest monuments of the Gampola period.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gadaladeniya-vihara.jpg" alt="Gadaladeniya Vihara" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Cherubino · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gadaladeniya_Viharaya_02.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Gadaladeniya Vihara, also called Saddharmatilaka Vihara or Dharma Kirthi Viharaya, stands at Pilimathalawa, about 12.5 km west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kandy">Kandy</a> and 3 km from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lankatilaka-vihara">Lankatilaka Vihara</a>. It was built under King Bhuvanekabahu IV, who reigned from 1341 to 1351, by the monk Seelavamsa Dharmakirti, and a carved inscription on the rock outcrop beneath it records its construction. Its architect, Ganesvarachari from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-india">South India</a>, worked in the Dravidian manner and joined it with Sinhalese architecture of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polonnaruwa">Polonnaruwa</a> era and other Indo Chinese patterns.</p><p>Among the scholar monks who lived there were Sangharaja Dharmakirti II, author of the Sangharaja-Nikāya, and Vimalakirti I. The temple was later abandoned until King Vira Parakrama Narendra Sinha, who reigned from 1707 to 1739, gave it to Weliwita Sri Saranankara Thero, whose pupils have cared for it ever since.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The main shrine is built entirely of carved granite, apart from two brick shikharas, and rests on a massive stone slab. The shikharas over the sanctum begin as octagons and are given the features of a dagoba. Probably in the 18th or 19th century a tiered and tiled Sinhalese roof on brick piers and wooden pillars was added, hiding the flat roof and the towers.</p><p>A richly carved Makara Thorana, or dragon arch, decorated with Brahmā, Śakra, Saman, Natha, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maitreya">Maitreya</a> and other figures, frames the entrance to the shrine room. Inside sits a Buddha 2.43 m high in the meditation posture, flanked by four standing Buddha statues. On the north side is a devale now dedicated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a>; it was built for Varuna, but as Vishnu grew more popular with devotees the shrine passed to him.</p><p>In the middle of the compound stands a later shrine, the Vijayothpaya, named after the palace of the god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indra">Indra</a> and built by King Parakramabahu V. A central stupa about 12.3 m high on a square platform is covered by a four-sided roof on stone pillars, with four smaller stupas in the cardinal directions. Their shrine rooms once held the Four Heavenly Kings and now hold statues of the seated Buddha.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gadaladeniya Vihara” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The period produced fine Buddhist monuments, notably the <b>Gadaladeniya</b> and Lankatilaka temples, which blend Sinhalese and south Indian styles.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-gampola">Kingdom of Gampola</a></li></ul></section>
